  "strategy": "## Approach and Rationale\nTo achieve a better score than the previous sequence (1.504740), we need a fundamentally new approach that focuses on time-frequency efficiency. While the authors previously combined p-norm LBFGS, Nesterov LP, and local refinement at a fixed length of 960, our method will explicitly modify the length of the sequence and optimize for multiple Fourier configurations. \n\n**Mathematical insight:**\nThe objective function measures the ratio of (1) the convolution's maximum value and (2) the square of the sum of normalized coefficients. We note that this is approximately equivalent to minimizing the maximum output while maintaining a minimally effective length. By varying length systematically, we expose multiple configurations where the autocorrelation might be weaker.\n\n**Key improvement:**\nWe use a multi-resolution optimization strategy that combines short sequences (512 elements) with dynamic interpolation to longer sequences (up to 2048) while preserving the ability to compare against the previous solution. This avoids the local minima found by single-length optimization.\n\n## Mathematical Specification\nWe define the evaluation metric as:\nscore = (2 * n * max_{0<=k<2n-1} convolve(a,a)[k]) / (sum(a))^2\n\nWhere convolve is the full linear convolution (not circular) of a sequence normalized to sum 1. The algorithm must generate sequences with elements in the range (0, 1000] to pass the defensive checks.\n\nThe math is implemented via:\n- Discrete Fourier Transform (via FFT)\n- Linear interpolation for length adjustments\n- Gaussian smoothing for improved time-localization\n- CLARABEL-based optimization for inequality constraints\n\n## Implementation Procedure\nWe will implement a three-stage reductive strategy:\n\n1. **Initialization Phase** (Under 30 seconds):\n   - Start from a small sequence (\u2264512) using structures 1 and 4 from the previous program.\n   - Generate three initial sequences of different lengths:\n     - Uniform distribution \u00b1 0.1\n     - Gaussian distribution \u00d7 0.85 scale\n     - Exponential decay \u00d7 1.0 with 10% noise\n   - Clip all sequences to [0, 1000] and normalize to sum=1.\n\n2. **Iterative Improvement** (Gradually increasing evaluation frequency):\n   - For each candidate sequence, compute time-frequency targets (sum of coefficient magnitudes and their positions).\n   - Use CLARABEL to minimize:\n     ||a||_\u221e part while ||a||_1 is fixed (constraint-based optimization)\n     score(a) = (2*n_out * max(convolve(a,a,mode=\"full\"))) / (sum(a))^2\n   - Alternate outer- and inner- optimization between short and long sequences (augmenting lengths when their score outperforms current).\n\n3. **Final Optimization** (More evaluations with aggressive length exploration):\n   - Expand memory usage by processing larger sequences (\u2265768)\n   - Use high-precision searching (targeting 1e-5)\n   - Output the sequence that exhibits the lowest score (passed to evaluate_sequence for final check) with its original length.\n\n## Search Choices and Useful Variations\nWe use a fixed base time allocation strategy:\n\n- **Initial length trial set:** [512, 768, 960, 1024, 1280, 2048] (all lengths rounded to higher power-of-2)\n- **Length interpolation factor:** Use cubic interpolation for smoother transitions.\n- **Structural initialization variants:** We implement 3 main variants as before:\n   variant0 - uniform (np.linspace(0,1,n))\n   variant1 - cosine squared\n   variant2 - exponential envelope\n\nThe structural variant is applied in stages:\n\n1. Stage1: uniform-based only when n is small (\u2264512).\n2. Stage2: Gaussian-based for medium n (768 to 1024).\n3. Stage3: exponential-based for large n (\u22651280).\n\nWe use dimensions that are powers-of-2 to ensure FFT efficiency.\n\n## Compute Budget and Robustness\nWe allocate the 1800 seconds dynamically:\n\n- **Target Evaluation Times:** About 60% C++ solving (10 samples), 30% NLP optimization (15+ samples), 10% FFT/convolution computation (trivial for small n).\n- **Termination Conditions:**\n  - If within 2100 total computations *without any improvement*, restart with new random maps.\n  - If we reach 1800 wall time seconds, return the best solution found.\n  - If all sequences become unstable (NaN or inf), fallback to geometric progression fixed-length.\n\n## Validation and Return Contract\nWe must:\n- Always cluster at least one viable sequence (via fallback).\n- Ensure no output violates the evaluation function's defensive checks.\n- Use the original evaluate_sequence for final verification.\n\nThis will search across 6 lengths and utilize up to 18 optimization points, ensuring breadth without exceeding time limits.",
